 "title": "Down by the Old Mill Stream",
 "description": "The 1910 waltz in true barbershop: the lead sings the tune, the tenor rings above it, the baritone fills the chord and the bass holds the roots. The chorus, slow and free, with the others answering the lead's \"You were sixteen\" and \"my village queen\".",
 "language": "en",
 "difficulty": "intermediate",
 "genre": [
  "barbershop",
  "waltz",
  "american"
 ],
 "style": "free",
 "tempo": 96,
 "key": "A",
 "timeSignature": "3/4",
 "pickupBeats": 1.5,
 "credits": {
  "words": "Tell Taylor (1910)",
  "tune": "Tell Taylor (1910)",
  "arrangement": "Stackapella, barbershop voicing over the 1910 sheet music's own harmony"
 },
 "licence": {
  "words": "public domain: published 1910; Taylor died in 1937",
  "tune": "public domain: published 1910; Taylor died in 1937. Earl K. Smith's 1937 renewal claimed him as a co-author of words and music; the courts found that claim unproven (Jerry Vogel Music Co. v. Forster, 2d Cir. 1945). He was living in 1948; his death year (1954 in secondary sources) is unconfirmed.",
  "arrangement": "original, written for Stackapella. Accepted exceptions (singable.py): the bass's low E on \"ging-\", a semitone under the beginner bass range, is the root of the E7 chord under the baritone's D and the lead's G sharp; sung an octave up it would lie above the baritone and leave the chord without its root at the bottom; and the tenor drops a fifth onto the B7's seventh on \"old\" and leaps a third up on \"mill\", kept on purpose for the C7 and B7 ring that is the song (the singability pass, 2026-11-01).",
  "recordings": "synthesized guides",
  "checked": null,
  "source": "melody, words and harmony from a later printing of the 1910 sheet music (Tell Taylor, Music Publisher, Chicago; copyright 1910, printed 1912 or later; not the first edition, whose deposit copies also printed Earl K. Smith's name), chorus \"Valse lento\", printed in G and written here in A at men's pitch (a semitone below our first draft, so the lead sits in a beginner's range and the bass goes no lower than its low E); read from the Connecticut College copy, scan at archive.org/details/sm_downbytheoldmillstream"
 },
